@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1866 = private unnamed_addr constant [1409 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for global variable names that follow the `mixedCase` convention.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends that global variable names should be lower case and\0Aseparated by underscores (also known as `snake_case`).\0A\0A> ### Global Variable Names\0A> (Let\E2\80\99s hope that these variables are meant for use inside one module\0A> only.) The conventions are about the same as those for functions.\0A>\0A> Modules that are designed for use via from M import * should use the\0A> __all__ mechanism to prevent exporting globals, or use the older\0A> convention of prefixing such globals with an underscore (which you might\0A> want to do to indicate these globals are \E2\80\9Cmodule non-public\E2\80\9D).\0A>\0A> ### Function and Variable Names\0A> Function names should be lowercase, with words separated by underscores\0A> as necessary to improve readability.\0A>\0A> Variable names follow the same convention as function names.\0A>\0A> mixedCase is allowed only in contexts where that\E2\80\99s already the prevailing\0A> style (e.g. threading.py), to retain backwards compatibility.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0AmyVariable = \22hello\22\0Aanother_variable = \22world\22\0Ayet_anotherVariable = \22foo\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Amy_variable = \22hello\22\0Aanother_variable = \22world\22\0Ayet_another_variable = \22foo\22\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.ignore-names`\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.extend-ignore-names`\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#global-variable-names\0A", align 1
