@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1884 = private unnamed_addr constant [729 x i8] c"## Removal\0AThis rule was removed from Ruff because it was common for it to introduce behavioral changes.\0ASee [#9007](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/9007) for more information.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the known pre-Python 2.5 ternary syntax.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APrior to the introduction of the if-expression (ternary) operator in Python\0A2.5, the only way to express a conditional expression was to use the `and`\0Aand `or` operators.\0A\0AThe if-expression construct is clearer and more explicit, and should be\0Apreferred over the use of `and` and `or` for ternary expressions.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ax, y = 1, 2\0Amaximum = x >= y and x or y\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ax, y = 1, 2\0Amaximum = x if x >= y else y\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1885 = private unnamed_addr constant [1245 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for dictionary unpacking in a for loop without calling `.items()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen iterating over a dictionary in a for loop, if a dictionary is unpacked\0Awithout calling `.items()`, it could lead to a runtime error if the keys are not\0Aa tuple of two elements.\0A\0AIt is likely that you're looking for an iteration over (key, value) pairs which\0Acan only be achieved when calling `.items()`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adata = {\22Paris\22: 2_165_423, \22New York City\22: 8_804_190, \22Tokyo\22: 13_988_129}\0A\0Afor city, population in data:\0A    print(f\22{city} has population {population}.\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adata = {\22Paris\22: 2_165_423, \22New York City\22: 8_804_190, \22Tokyo\22: 13_988_129}\0A\0Afor city, population in data.items():\0A    print(f\22{city} has population {population}.\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Known problems\0AIf the dictionary key is a tuple, e.g.:\0A\0A```python\0Ad = {(1, 2): 3, (3, 4): 5}\0Afor x, y in d:\0A    print(x, y)\0A```\0A\0AThe tuple key is unpacked into `x` and `y` instead of the key and values. This means that\0Athe suggested fix of using `d.items()` would result in different runtime behavior. Ruff\0Acannot consistently infer the type of a dictionary's keys.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0ADue to the known problem with tuple keys, this fix is unsafe.\0A", align 1
